-- title:
--   Lemma 3.5 — Conditional evaluation probabilities
-- statement:
--   Let $G$ be a finite simple undirected labeled graph, let $v$ be its fixed query root, and use the cached, early-stopping greedy evaluation. Write $R$ for its fresh evaluated vertices, $U=|R|$, $\tau(u)$ for elimination times, $M_s=\sum_{u\in R}\tau(u)^s$, $X=\sum_{u\in R\setminus\{v\}}\tau(u)^s$, $D=\sum_{u\in R\setminus\{v\}}\tau(u)^s\ln(r(u)/\tau(u))$, and $c_s=\ln(s-1)-1$. Unless stated otherwise below, the root has rank $1$ and the other ranks are independent uniforms on $(0,1)$.
--
--   Let $W=R\setminus(I_G(r)\cup\{v\})$. Reveal the accepted history and then all rejected nonroot ranks in decreasing elimination-time stages $D_t$, as in the revelation definition. At every stage, with pre-stage sigma-algebra $\mathcal G_t$, for $x\in D_t$ set
--
--   $$m_x=\max(\{t\}\cup\{\tau(u):u\in P_t\cap N_G(x)\}),\qquad \pi_x=\frac{m_x-t}{1-t}.$$
--
--   Both quantities have $\mathcal G_t$-measurable versions, and, on the distinct-rank domain,
--
--   $$x\in W\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad r(x)\le m_x.$$
--
--   Conditionally on $\mathcal G_t$, the indicators of $x\in W$ for $x\in D_t$ are jointly independent Bernoulli variables with means $\pi_x$. Equivalently the conditional probability of every Boolean pattern is the product of its Bernoulli factors. The generated sigma-algebra is a sub-sigma-algebra of the rank space.
--
--   This gives the full conditional joint law needed for all revelation stages, including the empty-stage case.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: finite labeled graph and real rank assignment → Fin n, lexicographic real-rank/label keys, and computed cached execution; reason: zero-based labels enumerate exactly the same finite graphs and implement the source tie rule without adding a hypothesis
--   - encoding: conditioning on h and previously revealed ranks; variables indexed by the random D_t → the sigma-algebra generated by labeled observation vectors; measurable versions modulo null sets and conditional expectations of all masked Boolean patterns; reason: the vectors encode exactly the same information; the fixed-carrier masking adds only deterministic zero coordinates and makes no singleton-history conditioning assumption
-- source:
--   Peter Kiss, Yuichi Yoshida, The Local Probe Complexity of Maximal Independent Set, STOC 2027 submission #871 (October 2026), p. 14, PDF p. 16, Lemma 3.5; https://github.com/sigact-stoc/stoc2027/releases/download/papers-1/871.pdf

import Definitions.Def_LocalProbeMIS871_Greedy_Revelation

open MeasureTheory
open scoped BigOperators Classical
noncomputable section
set_option autoImplicit false

namespace LocalProbeMIS871.Greedy

/-- Lemma 3.5, printed p.14 / PDF16. Full joint law via all Boolean patterns;
masked coordinates outside D_t are deterministically zero, including padded stages. -/
theorem lemma_3_5 {n : ℕ} (G : SimpleGraph (Fin n)) (v : Fin n) (i : ℕ) :
    stageSigma G v i ≤ (inferInstance : MeasurableSpace (Fin n → ℝ)) ∧
    (∀ x : Fin n, ∃ mx pix : (Fin n → ℝ) → ℝ,
      Measurable[stageSigma G v i] mx ∧ Measurable[stageSigma G v i] pix ∧
      mx =ᵐ[forcedLaw v] stageM G v i x ∧ pix =ᵐ[forcedLaw v] stagePi G v i x) ∧
    (∀ r, GoodRanks v r → ∀ x ∈ stage G v i r,
      (x ∈ W G v r ↔ r x ≤ stageM G v i x r)) ∧
    (∀ b : Fin n → Bool,
      (forcedLaw v)[stagePattern G v i b | stageSigma G v i] =ᵐ[forcedLaw v]
        patternMass G v i b) := by sorry

end LocalProbeMIS871.Greedy
